      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\u222d}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(3 x + 1)+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(3z)+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}(xy) \\\\  & =3+0+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={\u222d}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}3\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[3x]_{x=0}^{x=4}\\cdot (3-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(12-0)\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =72.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
